@startuml
title collab Semantic Model — Draft 3
left to right direction
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam linetype ortho

package "Design / Structural Model" {
  class Participant {
    +name
    +kind : ParticipantKind
    +multiplicity
  }
  enum ParticipantKind {
    ACTIVE_OBJECT
    PASSIVE_HSM
    ISR
    DEVICE_DRIVER
    TIMER
    TASK_OR_THREAD
    OTHER
  }
  class Collaboration {
    +isReflexive : Boolean <<derived>>
  }
  class Route {
    +isAvailable : Boolean <<runtime/derived>>
  }
  class Signal {
    +name
  }

  Participant --> ParticipantKind : kind
  Participant "0..*" -- "0..*" Participant : collaborates with
  (Participant, Participant) .. Collaboration
  Collaboration "1" *-- "1..*" Route : defines
  Participant "1" <-- "0..*" Route : origin
  Participant "1" <-- "0..*" Route : destination
}

package "Runtime / Dynamic Model" {
  class Event {
    +generatedAt
    +parameters [0..*]
  }
  class Injection {
    +mechanism : InjectionMechanism
    +queueOrder : QueueOrder [0..1]
  }
  enum InjectionMechanism {
    POST
    PUBLISH
    DISPATCH
  }
  enum QueueOrder {
    FIFO
    LIFO
  }
  class Delivery {
    +deliveredAt
  }

  Injection --> InjectionMechanism : mechanism
  Injection --> QueueOrder : queueOrder\n{POST only}
}

Event "0..*" --> "1" Signal : type
Participant "1" <-- "0..*" Event : generated by
Event "1" -- "1" Injection : injected by
Injection "1" -- "0..*" Delivery : results in
Route "1" <-- "0..*" Delivery : traverses
Participant "1" <-- "0..*" Delivery : received by

note right of Participant
A Participant is an identifiable system element
that can originate and/or receive Events and
therefore take part in Collaborations.

Examples include Active Objects, passive HSMs,
ISRs, timers, device drivers, and tasks/threads.

Detailed capabilities and constraints associated
with each kind remain to be modelled.
end note

note top of Collaboration
Collaboration is the association class for the
reflexive "collaborates with" relationship.

Collaboration(P1,P2) = Collaboration(P2,P1)

The relationship itself is not directional.

isReflexive is derived:
  true  when P1 = P2
  false when P1 != P2
end note

note bottom of Route
A Route is a directional architectural path
from an origin Participant to a destination
Participant through which Events may be delivered.

Route(P1 -> P2) != Route(P2 -> P1)

A Route belongs to the design-time possibility
topology. Its availability may vary at runtime,
for example with P/S subscription state.
end note

note right of Signal
A Signal identifies the semantic type of an Event.

A Signal belongs to the design-time event
vocabulary; individual Events occur at runtime.
end note

note bottom of Event
An Event is a single runtime occurrence generated
by a Participant at an instant in time.

It has exactly one Signal and may carry zero or
more parameters.

For P/S, this draft treats publication as one
Event followed by zero or more Deliveries of
that same Event.
end note

note bottom of Injection
Injection describes how an Event enters the
event-delivery mechanism.

POST:
  targets an AO queue; FIFO or LIFO

PUBLISH:
  names no individual receiver; subscription
  state determines zero or more Deliveries

DISPATCH:
  synchronously dispatches to a passive HSM in
  the current run-to-completion context
end note

note bottom of Delivery
A Delivery is one runtime arrival of an Event
at a receiving Participant.

Each Delivery traverses exactly one Route.

One Injection may produce:
  POST      -> normally one Delivery
  DISPATCH  -> normally one Delivery
  PUBLISH   -> zero, one, or many Deliveries
end note

legend bottom
Draft 3 deliberately omits EventFlow.

Open questions:
* static Signal-to-Route permissions;
* participant capabilities and legal delivery mechanisms;
* dynamic subscription/route availability;
* Event/Injection cardinality;
* treatment of self-events in system-level views.
endlegend

@enduml
